Subject: [PATCH] Every window of the hall is found and lights its own way

Patch 0032 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

The user's test of 0031 in the Temple of Time, 2026-10-08: the walls' shadow worked, but the
hall's side windows gave no light and "look like they're actually just casting shadows onto the
walls as if they're an object hanging in front of the wall", and a bright spot on the floor sat
away from where the light falls.

The side windows are an actor's (Bg_Toki_Hikari), drawn in ONE draw: four windows on the two side
walls and one at the front, five separate pieces facing three ways. A draw was taken as a window
only when the whole of it was one flat plane, so these never were, and their glass, hung a little
in front of the wall, cast the room's light onto the wall below. And the glass an adult sees is a
darker texture than a child's (0.38 and 0.23 against 0.71 and 0.33), under the lattice test's bar.

So a draw is split into its pieces, the triangles joined by a shared corner, and each piece is
measured as a window of its own; the lattice test takes a mean of 0.3 and a spread of 0.2; a draw
with a window in it takes the foliage mask, seen by every gathering ray and by no shadow ray, so
the glass casts no shadow and keeps its place in the raster; and a point on the glass counts as
lit, so the windows stay bright.

With every window on the one sun of 0031, the side windows faced across a light falling lengthwise
and let almost none of it in. So each window falls inward and down at its own slope again; the
window a drawn shaft comes through takes the shaft's direction, kept by where its glass is; and the
walls' shadow is one even share over every surface of the place, carried by the first window. No
shaft lights on its own in a place with windows, which takes away the bright spot. Up to twelve
beams and windows follow the game's lights, and every four seconds or so the log says how many
windows and shafts were found and whether a shaft was traced to a window.

